Compare the requested scope to deliver=true drafting entries, not just the research.
Only deliver=true outputs reach the document, verbatim and in list order. Check
that evidence notes are excluded and every final chapter, introduction and
conclusion needed by the request is selected. Assembly does not rewrite them.
For "all" or chronological collections, check BOTH endpoints, omitted items,
duplicates, and whether ranges agree with their named items. A plan that stops
partway through the requested collection is incomplete even if every listed
step looks reasonable. Research coverage without matching drafting coverage
does not deliver the requested document. Check that requested depth is retained
across the batches and that drafting receives the evidence it needs.

Do not approve because the plan says it is complete. Identify specific gaps.
Read the actual drafting instructions before claiming an item is omitted;
abbreviations and established short names count when their identity is clear.
Repeated names are not automatically duplicates: distinct terms, periods or
events involving the same subject can be necessary to chronological coverage.
Do not replace an explicit named sequence with a count recalled from memory.
Do not penalize the absence of a file-saving step: final delivery handles that.
If chronology or an up-to-date roster is uncertain, require a bounded step to
verify it and make its result available to the subsequent research and drafts.

Keep the verdict concise. Return ONLY JSON: {"ok":true,"message":""} if it covers the goal, otherwise
